<seg id="s0004" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">required assistance for many activities of daily living.
Serologic assay for anti-GAD 65 antibodies was nega-
tive. The patient was attempting to conceive during
the initial neurologic evaluation and as a result, refused
treatment with benzodiazepines and immunoglobulin
due to concerns of fetal toxicity.
The patient had type I diabetes mellitus initially
diagnosed at age 15, which was managed with a subcu-
taneous insulin pump. Her diabetes was well controlled
(HbA1C 5.2%). A previous gastric emptying study iden-
tiﬁed gastroparesis, but the patient denied symptoms of
aspiration or regurgitation. She reported blunted aware-
ness to hypoglycemic events and a stocking-and-glove
peripheral neuropathy. Her renal function was normal.
She had asthma requiring occasional albuterol use,
allergies to several medications including sodium citrate
and citrate fruits (severe dermal and gastrointestinal
tract ulcers), and intolerance to metoclopramide.
Physical examination revealed bilateral ﬂexion
contractures of the ﬁngers, elbows and shoulders. The
patient’s lower extremities remained extended and
abducted in the sitting position on an examination table.
Her motor strength was intact throughout and reﬂexes
were normal bilaterally. Exaggerated lumbar lordosis
and neuraxial stiffness were absent. A Mallampati grade
II airway examination with normal thyromental dis-
tance was observed. Anesthesia records from our institu-
tion documented that a grade 1 direct laryngoscopy view
was present during previous tracheal intubations.
The obstetrical and anesthesiology teams, a neurol-
ogy consultant and the patient concurred that cesarean
delivery was most appropriate because the possibility
for lower extremity muscle spasms during induction of
labor made vaginal delivery inadvisable. In-depth dis-
cussions were conducted between the patient and the
care team about her anesthetic and surgical options.
Considering the patient’s SPS and preeclampsia, a
multidisciplinary consensus decision was reached to
provide slowly titrated epidural anesthesia for cesarean
delivery. The patient and the consultants agreed that
general anesthesia would be used in the event that emer-
gent delivery was required.
Twelve days after admission, a cesarean delivery was
urgently scheduled because of recurrent fetal heart rate
decelerations. In addition, the patient’s hypertension
had become more refractory to treatment, and reversal
of end-diastolic umbilical arterial blood ﬂow velocity
was observed. The patient received famotidine 20 mg,
and her insulin infusion pump was temporarily discon-
tinued. The patient was taken to the operating room,
and with the obstetricians present, the patient was posi-
tioned in the right lateral position before lumbar epidu-
ral catheter placement. A ﬂuid bolus of lactated Ringer’s
solution was administered. Good communication before
and during the procedure reduced the patient’s anxiety
and minimized the risk of stimulated muscle spasms.
Fetal heart rate was continuously monitored; conversion
to general anesthesia was planned if fetal heart rate
decelerations recurred. Moderate sedation was not used
to avoid neonatal respiratory depression. A 17-gauge
Tuohy needle was inserted at the L4–5 interspace after
local anesthetic inﬁltration and the epidural space was
identiﬁed on the ﬁrst attempt using saline for loss of
resistance. A 20-gauge multi-oriﬁce epidural catheter
was inserted 6 cm into the epidural space. The patient</seg>
<seg id="s0005" group="l0001" targets="p1" role="patient_specific">was then positioned supine with left uterine displace-
ment. An epidural test dose (1.5% lidocaine with
1:200 000 epinephrine 3 mL) was negative for tachycar-
dia, transient neurologic ﬁndings (tinnitus, perioral
numbness) and lower extremity weakness. A total of
20 mL of 2% lidocaine with 1:200 000 epinephrine and
fentanyl 5 lg/mL was administered in 5 mL increments
over 15 min to achieve a T6 block as determined by sen-
sory dermatomal pinprick assessment. The patient did
not develop muscle spasms and the fetal heart rate
remained reassuring throughout the procedure.
The uneventful cesarean delivery was performed
through a classical incision. A 430 g male infant was
delivered with Apgar scores of 4, 5, and 6 at 1, 5, and
10 min, respectively; the baby was transferred to the
neonatal intensive care unit for further care. The mother
received two 5-mL epidural doses of 0.5% bupivacaine
45 min after the initial block because of the duration
of the procedure. She also received intravenous fentanyl
50 lg because of discomfort during removal of the pla-
centa and manual exploration of the uterine cavity to
evaluate a small area of hemorrhage noted on the supe-
rior aspect of the placenta. A 20 U/L oxytocin infusion
was started after the placenta was delivered. The patient
remained hemodynamically stable and did not suffer
muscle spasms during the operation. As is our standard
practice, we discussed neuraxial opioids or patient-
controlled analgesia as options for postoperative pain
control with the patient before surgery. She preferred
and received patient-controlled analgesia (intermittent
bolus hydromorphone). Intravenous ketorolac and oral
acetaminophen were used as adjunctive medications for
postoperative analgesia. The patient had no further SPS
symptoms or muscle spasms throughout her hospitaliza-
tion. The remainder of her course was uncomplicated,
and she was discharged on the fourth postoperative
day. The premature infant had a prolonged intensive
care unit stay, but was eventually discharged home.</seg>
Discussion
We chose epidural anesthesia (lidocaine with epinephr-
ine and fentanyl) for our patient with SPS and
preeclampsia undergoing urgent cesarean delivery
because general anesthesia may be associated with
delayed emergence and prolonged muscle weakness in
patients with SPS. This approach provided suitable
86 International Journal of Obstetric Anesthesia

operating conditions to facilitate prompt delivery of the
baby. We decided against using spinal or combined
spinal-epidural anesthesia because our patient had sig-
niﬁcant neurologic sequelae of type I diabetes mellitus
(peripheral neuropathy, attenuated awareness to the
presence of hypoglycemia) consistent with autonomic
nervous system dysfunction. Autonomic dysfunction
may occur in SPS.
17 In addition, she had chronic
essential hypertension and superimposed preeclampsia.
We were concerned that the combination of these fac-
tors would result in hemodynamic instability during
the rapid onset of sympathetic nerve block associated
with spinal anesthesia. Our patient chose patient-
controlled analgesia supplemented with intravenous
ketorolac and oral acetaminophen over epidural opioids
for postoperative analgesia, although the latter option
does not appear contraindicated in parturients with SPS.
Treatment of SPS is directed towards facilitating
GABA-mediated transmission (e.g. benzodiazepines),
reducing muscle spasticity (e.g. baclofen), and
immunomodulation in anti-GAD 65 antibody-positive
patients (e.g. plasmapheresis, intravenous immunoglob-
ulin, corticosteroids).
2,4 The three previous case reports
that described the peripartum care of women with SPS
provided few details about anesthetic management.
14–16
Epidural analgesia for labor was described in a woman
with SPS who had been treated with diazepam and
baclofen. This patient developed muscle spasms during
repair of an episiotomy after a forceps-assisted deliv-
ery.
14 Unfortunately, this report did not provide any
additional details about the epidural anesthetic. Labor
epidural analgesia was also used for a woman with SPS
whose diagnosis was established during her pregnancy.
16
Late decelerations occurred at 7–8 cm cervical dilation,
and an urgent cesarean delivery was performed using
epidural anesthesia, but the speciﬁcs of the anesthetic
management were not provided.
16 Cesarean delivery
under general anesthesia of a term infant was described
in another patient with SPS, details about her anesthetic
management again were not reported. 15
Volatile anesthetics may exacerbate abnormal
GABA-mediated neurotransmission and may lead to
hypotonia in SPS.
17 Volatile agents are known to cause
muscular relaxation and potentiate the effects of non-
depolarizing neuromuscular blockers through their inhi-
bitory actions on GABA
B receptors.18 Johnson et al.
described a woman with SPS undergoing repair of an
intrathecal baclofen pump. 8 Anesthesia was induced
using thiopental, sufentanil, and vecuronium and main-
tained using isoﬂurane, nitrous oxide and oxygen.
Prolonged emergence and weakness were observed, but
these complications likely resulted from the combination
of residual neuromuscular blockade and subcutaneous
inﬁltration of thiopental (a plasma thiopental concentra-
tion obtained six hours after anesthetic induction was
10 lg/mL) and not because of an exacerbation of the
patient’s underlying SPS. The patient underwent a
subsequent uncomplicated isoﬂurane anesthetic in the
absence of neuromuscular blockade.
8 Prolonged weak-
ness after an isoﬂurane-sufentanil-atracurium anesthetic
in a woman with SPS undergoing a colon resection was
reported despite normal atracurium pharmacokinetics.
9
The patient’s weakness was attributed to isoﬂurane-
induced GABA
B inhibition, but chronic baclofen use
may have been a contributing factor because baclofen
may cause hypotonia during anesthesia independent of
SPS.
19
In contrast, other authors have demonstrated that
volatile anesthetics do not cause prolonged weakness
when used without neuromuscular blockers in patients
with SPS.
11,12 In two other patients with SPS, total
intravenous anesthesia consisting of propofol and
remifentanil was successfully used without a neuromus-
cular blocker.
10,13 When considered collectively, these
data suggest that volatile or intravenous anesthesia
would have been a reasonable option for our patient’s
cesarean delivery if the heart rate decelerations recurred
or the epidural anesthetic was inadequate. Nevertheless,
a rapid-sequence induction would most likely have been
indicated for tracheal intubation considering the urgent
nature of the procedure, her pregnancy, and diabetes-
induced gastroparesis, which substantially increased
her risk of acid aspiration.
During the patient’s initial admission multidisci-
plinary discussions that included the patient, it was
decided that if general anesthesia were necessary, a
modiﬁed rapid-sequence induction with high-dose
rocuronium would be used. The patient was aware that
this likely would result in prolonged intubation and
mechanical ventilation following the cesarean delivery.
At the time of her delivery, sugammadex was not avail-
able for use. Although an awake intubation without the
use of any neuromuscular blockade may have been
preferable, the plan to only use general anesthesia in
the event of an emergency made this option less feasible.
Use of succinylcholine as part of a rapid-sequence
induction may have been inadvisable in our patient con-
sidering prolonged bed rest during her admission and
the chronic muscle stimulation that affects SPS patients.
However, successful use of succinylcholine was reported
previously in a pediatric SPS patient without causing
hyperkalemia or prolonged neuromuscular blockade.
20
As our care of the current patient demonstrates,
regional and neuraxial anesthetic techniques have been
successfully used for many procedures including cesar-
ean delivery in patients with SPS.
12–14,16 Some authors
have advocated the use of these techniques to avoid
postoperative hypotonia associated with volatile anes-
thetics or neuromuscular blockers.
11,12 Nevertheless,
muscle spasms may be precipitated by painful stimuli,
such as needle insertion and local anesthetic injection,
in patients with SPS. Clear communication and judi-
International Journal of Obstetric Anesthesia 87

cious use of moderate sedation are recommended to
reduce the potential for muscle spasms while performing
regional or neuraxial anesthesia.
11 Our patient refused
sedatives because she was concerned about the effects
of these medications on the fetus, so we were very clear
and reassuring with her about each aspect of the epidu-
ral procedure. Neuraxial stiffness or exaggerated lumbar
lordosis resulting from tonic muscle contraction may
also make placement of a spinal or epidural needle more
difﬁcult in some patients with SPS, but our patient did
not have these major axial ramiﬁcations of SPS.
Anesthetic management of a parturient with SPS may
be challenging. As with most complex and rare diseases,
planning with a multidisciplinary team and patient
involvement will lead to the best possible outcomes for
both mother and baby.
